Abstract
Corneal epithelial stem cells residing within the annular limbal crypts regulate adult tissue homeostasis. Autologous limbal grafts and tissue engineered corneal epithelial cell sheets have been widely used in the treatment of various ocular surface defects. In case of bilateral limbal defects, pluripotent stem cell (PSC) derived corneal epithelial cells are now being explored as an alternative to allogeneic limbal grafts. We report here an efficient method to generate complex three dimensional corneal organoids from human PSCs. The eye field primordial (EFP) clusters that emerged from differentiating PSCs developed into whole eye ball-like, self-organized, three dimensional, miniature structures consisting of retinal primordia (RP), corneal primordia (CP), primitive eye lid-like outer covering and ciliary margin zone-like adnexal tissues in a step-wise maturation process within 15 weeks. These minicorneal organoids recapitulate the early developmental events in vitro and displayed similar anatomical features and marker expression profiles as that of adult corneal tissues and offers an alternative tissue source for regenerating different layers of the cornea and eliminates the need for complicated cell enrichment procedures.
## Introduction
Cornea is the transparent and avascular tissue on the ocular surface, through which the light enters the eye. Any damage to its epithelial, stromal or endothelial cell layers can lead to visual impairment. The annular limbus surrounding the cornea harbors adult stem cells that regenerate different parts of the cornea (Schermer et al., 1986; Cotsarelis et al., 1989). Cell replacement therapy using autologous or allogeneic, adult limbal grafts has been the standard of care in the treatment of patients with severe limbal stem cell deficiency (LSCD) (Rama et al., 2010; Sangwan et al., 2011; Basu et al., 2016). However, in case of bilateral epithelial defects, and for the treatment of conditions affecting the stromal and endothelial cell layers, alternate stem cell sources such as the embryonic stem cells (ESCs) and induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs) have been explored to generate various corneal cell types (Ahmad et al., 2007; Shalom-Feuerstein et al., 2012; Hayashi et al., 2012; Sareen et al., 2014; Mikhailova et al., 2014; Chan et al., 2013; Zhang et al., 2014; Chen et al., 2015; McCabe et al., 2015).
A recent report have shown coordinated development of corneal epithelium, neural crest cells, lens epithelium and retinal cells from iPSCs, in a 2D culture system and employed FACS sorting to establish pure cultures of corneal epithelial cells (Hayashi et al., 2016). However, the requirement of rigorous cell enrichment protocols are a major hurdle in tissuespecific cell expansion and can be overcome by establishing three dimensional (3D) culture systems. This method exploits the inherent self organizing capacity of differentiating progenitor cell populations, together with the surrounding niche cells, to generate complex tissue structures in vitro. This has been demonstrated successfully with the generation of neuro-retinal tissues using PSCs (Eiraku et al., 2011; Gonzalez-Cordero et al., 2013; Assawachananont et al., 2014; Reichman et al., 2014; Zhong et al., 2014; Hiler et al., 2015; Kaewkhaw et al., 2015; Völkner et al., 2016). A latest report has just described a method of generating immature cornea organoid from human iPSCs and shown them to express few corneal markers (Foster et al., 2017). We report here a much simpler and efficient culture method that can generate complex 3D corneal organoids both from human ESCs and iPSCs. We report the establishment of long term cultures and characterized these organoids at different stages of maturation.  The mature organoids developed into complex, multilayered, minicornea-like three dimensional tissues and recapitulated the early developmental events in vitro. We also show that they offer an alternative tissue source for various ocular cell types and report the generation of transplantable sheets of corneal epithelium suitable for regenerative applications.
## METHODS
The study was approved by our Institutional Review Board (IRB) and all research involving human samples followed the tenets of the Declaration of Helsinki. Experiments involving animals were conducted in adherence to the ARVO statement for use of animals and with the approval of the Institutional Animal Ethics Committee (AEC).
## Derivation and maintenance of human iPSCs
Full thickness punch biopsies of skin were taken from volunteers with their informed consent. The biopsies were used to establish human dermal fibroblast (HDFs) cultures. Retroviral cocktail containing individual vectors expressing the OSKM transgenes were used to transduce passage 3 HDFs at an approximate MOI of 2. The cells were then split and cultured under standard human ESC culture conditions. The reprogrammed clones that emerged after 3 weeks were manually picked based on colony morphology and five clones were passaged for further expansion. The clones were also adapted to feeder free culture conditions on Matrigel™ (Corning, USA) coated plates using mTeSR™1 kit, as per manufacturer’s instructions (STEMCELL Technologies, USA). The reprogramming efficiency was $0.005\%$ and the clone hiPSC-F2-3F1 was expanded beyond 25 passages and characterized for the stemness and pluripotency.
## Eye field differentiation of human iPSCs and hESCs
Growing cultures of human ESC line, BJNhem20 and the normal human iPSCs line, hiPSCF2-3F1 were differentiated towards eye field commitment as described below. When the cultures reached $70\mathrm{-}80\%$ confluence, the growth medium was replaced with differentiation medium (DM: DMEM/F12, $4\%$ KOSR, $4\%$ FBS, 1X NEAA, 1X Glutamax, 1X Pen-Strep) (Thermo Fisher Scientific inc. USA) to induce spontaneous differentiation for 2 days. Subsequently, the cultures were shifted to retinal differentiation medium (RDM: $\mathrm{DM}+2\%$ B27) and maintained for a month to induce eye field specification. Noggin was omitted in the RDM cocktail for uninhibited $\mathrm{TGF\upbeta}$ and BMP intrinsic signals that directs ocular surface ectoderm development. The distinct eye field primordial (EFP) clusters that emerged at 4 weeks were either continued as adherent cultures in situ or excised manually for suspension cultures as described below.
## Corneal differentiation of eye field clusters
The EFPs were further continued in situ as adherent cultures in RDM for another 4 weeks to allow whole eye ball like structure development, with transparent corneal primordia on the surface and neuro-retinal cup on the basal side. These cultures were further maintained in corneal differentiation medium (CDM: $\mathrm{DM}+1\%\mathrm{N}2+5\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Insulin $-\ 5\mathrm{ng/mL}$ FGF + 10 $\mathrm{\ng/mL}$ EGF) (Thermo Fisher Scientific inc. USA) for further 6-8 weeks to enable further maturation of the ocular surface structures. However, majority of the EFPs gave rise to concentric cell outgrowths as described previously (Hayashi et al., 2016). Alternatively, the EFPs were manually scooped out intact and cultured in RDM for 4 weeks in non-adherent plates. It is critical to excise the EFPs at 4 weeks before the commencement of initial waves of surface ectodermal and neural crest cell migration. Within 2 weeks of suspension culture, distinct retinal primordial (RP) and transparent, bubble-like corneal primordial (CP) structures emerge from the floating EFPs. At 6-8 weeks of differentiation, the delicate CPs were dissected out of floating EFPs and cultured separately for further 8-10 weeks in CDM for tissue maturation, as depicted in Fig 1A. Alternatively, the MCs were processed directly for explants cultures or RNA isolation or fixed in $10\%$ formalin for IHC.
## Explant culture of minicorneas
Minicorneas at different stages of maturation (6 weeks to 10 weeks) were taken and the basal stalk that carries the niche-like organizer along with the adjoining epithelium was chopped out under a microscope and cut into fine pieces in few drops of CDM. The tissue explants were picked by a needle and explanted on to the surface of de-epithelialized human amniotic membranes (hAM). Alternatively, the explants were also placed on Matrigel coated glass coverslips. The cultures were maintained in CDM and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $5\%$ CO2 supply. The epithelial cells migrate out of the explants and form growth zones that merge with each other to form uniform epithelial sheets within 10 days. The cell sheets were fixed and processed for IHC as described below.
## Genomic PCR and Semiquantitative reverse transcription PCR
Genomic DNA and total RNA were isolated from cell samples using standard procedures. cDNAs were prepared by reverse transcription using SuperScript $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ II RT kit (Invitrogen, Life Technologies Corporation). PCRs were performed for all the genes tested, using either the genomic DNA or cDNA as reaction templates $(\mathrm{n}{=}3)$ . Template concentrations were normalized based on eEF1α expression. Table S1 summarizes the primers used in the study.
The amplicons were resolved on $1\%$ (w/v) agarose gels, stained with ethidium bromide and imaged using Gel Doc™ $\mathrm{XR+}$ System (BioRad, USA).
## Immunohistochemistry and image analysis
The minicorneas were fixed in $10\%$ formalin and paraffin embedded for further sectioning. Thin $4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ sections were taken and processed for evaluation by H&E, PAS and Alcian blue staining by following standard procedures. For IHCs, antigen retrieval was done by heating with sodium citrate buffer and the slides were processed for blocking and antibody incubations.  DAB staining of samples were done as per manufacturer’s instructions (Super SensitiveTM One-Step Polymer-HRP IHC detection System, Biogenex) and counter stained with either hematoxylin or PI $(1~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ or DAPI $\mathrm{1~\upmug/mL})$ . Table S2 summarizes the details of antibodies used in the study. Alkaline phosphatase staining was done as per manufacturer’s instruction (Chemicon, Millipore Corporation, MA, USA). The samples were finally mounted with DPX or glycerol and imaged using an epifluorescence microscope (Olympus IX71, Japan) or a confocal microscope (LSM 510, Carl Zeiss, Inc., NY, USA). The images were analyzed using the ImagePro Express and LSM 510 Meta, Version 3.2 software respectively and the composites were prepared using Adobe Photoshop CS.
## Teratoma formation assay
iPSCs at passage 25 were suspended in $20\%$ Matrigel in DMEM/F12 and kept on ice. About 1 million cells in $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ was aspirated into tuberculin syringes fitted with 26G needle and injected into the subcutaneous space above the rear right haunch of 6 week old Nude mice $\scriptstyle(\ n=8)$ ). Teratomas that developed at 6-8 weeks post-injection were surgically dissected after euthanizing the animals. The tissues were fixed overnight in $4\%$ paraformaldehyde and processed for paraffin embedding. The tissue blocks were sectioned and processed for IHC examination as described above.
## Karyotyping assay
The cells at passage 8 and 20 were grown under standard iPSC culture conditions. About 70- $80\%$ confluent cultures were treated with colcemid $(0.1\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ for 2-3 hrs to induce metaphase arrest and trypsinized to prepare single cell suspensions. The cells were further treated with a hypotonic solution, fixed and the samples were then dropped onto clean glass slides (Fisher Scientific, USA) and air dried. After a brief trypsin treatment, the chromosomes were G-banded by Giemsa staining. Well spread metaphases were imaged and analyzed using Cytovision-automated (Applied Imaging, Santa Barbara, CA, USA).
## Transmission electron microscopy
The tissues were fixed in $2.5\%$ gluteraldehyde in $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{M}}$ phosphate buffer $\mathrm{(pH}7.2)$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and washed with 1X PBS thoroughly and post fixed in $1\%$ aqueous Osmium Tetroxide for 2h. The samples were then washed, dehydrated in series of graded alcohols and embedded in Spurr’s resin and incubated at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ for complete polymerization. Ultra thin (60 nm) sections were taken using the ultra microtome (Leica Ultra cut UCT-GA-D/E-1/00), mounted on copper grids and stained with saturated aqueous urenyl acetate (UA) and counter stained with Reynolds lead citrate (LC). The sections were viewed under TEM (Model: Hitachi, H-7500 from JAPAN) at required magnifications.
Statistics: Test values were reported as mean values $\pm$ standard deviations.
## Results
## Derivation and characterization of human induced pluripotent stem cells
As pluripotent stem cells are valuable cell sources for generating various ocular cell types and to study organ development in vitro, we derived and characterized few human induced pluripotent stem cell lines from dermal fibroblasts (Takahashi et al., 2007). As shown in Fig. S1A, the hiPSC-F2-3F1 line formed typical embryonic stem cell-like colonies both on mouse embryonic fibroblast (MEF) feeders and on Matrigel coated surfaces. This line expanded well under standard hiPSC culture conditions and the cells were passaged more than 25 times. They remained pluripotent and expressed the stem cell markers, OCT4, SOX2, SSEA4 and alkaline phosphatase (Fig. S1B). When passage 25 cells were transplanted into the subcutaneous space of nude mice, they proliferated and developed into teratomas comprising of all three germ layers within 6 weeks ( $\mathrm{\tilde{n}}{=}8$ , 6/8 animals developed teratomas) (Fig. S1C). The gross karyotype of this female line was found to be normal at passage 20 (Fig. S1D). Genotype analysis confirmed the presence of integrated copies of the three transgenes, OCT4, SOX2, KLF4 but not cMYC. The endogenous copies of all four transgenes were active in passage 25 cells and were expressed at levels comparable to that of the hESC line, BJNhem20 (Inamdar et al., 2009; Mariappan et al., 2015) (Fig. S1E).
Eye field differentiation of pluripotent stem cells and development of corneal primordial structures
To induce ocular differentiation, the iPSCs and ESCs were grown to $70\mathrm{-}80\%$ confluence under feeder-free conditions and differentiation was initiated in situ as described in the Methods. At 4 weeks of differentiation, distinct clusters of raised, circular to oval-shaped eye field primordial (EFP) clusters were developed as shown in Fig. S2A. Starting from 1 million PSCs, an average of about $27.33{\scriptstyle\pm13.63}$ EFP clusters could be generated from one well of a 6-well plate $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}\mathbf{=}6)$ ). To further confirm that these are EFPs, we manually scooped out theses clusters for total RNA isolation and marker expression analysis by reverse transcription PCR. As shown in Fig. S2B, the expression of PAX6, OTX2, SIX6 and RX confirmed that these 3D clusters consisted of eye field committed progenitor cells. When the EFPs are allowed to differentiate further in situ, they gave rise to lens epithelial clusters (Fig. S2C) and a SEAM (self-formed ectodermal autonomous multi-zone) of ocular surface epithelium by 6-8 weeks, as described by Hayashi et al., 2016. The central island of neuro-retinal (NR) cells were $\mathrm{CHX10^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{RCVRN^{+}}$ and the SEAM of ocular surface ectodermal (OSE) sheets were ${\tt P}63^{+}$ (Fig. 1B, i-ii). A wave of $\mathrm{SOX10^{+}}$ pigmented neural crest cells (NCCs) marked the boundary between NR and OSE cell zones (Fig. 1B, iv). The retinal pigmented epithelial (RPE) cells emerged as compact, non-pigmented epithelium, all around the NR clusters and later matured to acquire pigmentation. Lentoid clusters expressing gamma crystallin developed at a precise location adjacent to NR clusters (Fig 1B, v, Fig. S2C). The NR clusters developed into optic cups and $\mathrm{CHX10^{+}}$ precursors self-organized to form the neuro-retinal layer (Fig. S2D). Pigmented melanocytes were also observed interspersed within the zone of migrating epithelial cells (Fig 1B, vi, Fig. S2E).
Apart from the emergence of SEAM zones, rare clusters of EFPs developed into three dimensional (3D), miniature eye balls, with transparent anterior segment-like structures on the surface and complex neuro-retinal structures at the bottom. A wave of pigmented neural crest cells set the boundary for the cornea-like structures (Fig 2A, iii-iv). When the EFPs at 4 weeks were manually scooped out and cultured under suspension in non-adherent dishes, about $40.05{\scriptstyle\pm3.89}$ percentage of EFPs gave rise to distinct corneal primordial (CP) structures apart from the generally observed retinal primordia (RP) $\scriptstyle(\mathtt{n}=6)$ (Fig 2A, vi-ix). In the earlier method described by Hayashi et al., 2016, the corneal epithelial cell enrichment was achieved by approximate zoning of cell outgrowths within the SEAM region and by FACS sorting of $\mathrm{SEA4+}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ITGB4+}$ cells. However, the method described here enables self-organization of different corneal primordial cells (OSE and neural crest cells) into 3D minicorneal organoids that can directly serve as valuable tissue sources to study corneal development in vitro and also to establish pure cultures of different corneal cell types.
## Morphological features of minicorneas
The minicorneas (MCs) ranged from about $1\ \mathrm{mm}$ to $7\ \mathrm{mm}$ in diameter (Table S3). A magnified view of MCs revealed the presence of a uniform epithelial cell lining (Fig 2A, ix, Video S1). Transmission electron microscopic images of an 8 weeks old minicornea revealed the presence of a layer of epithelium with tight junctions and numerous apical microvilli structures, which is a feature of corneal epithelium but not that of lens epithelial cells. The mucin secreting goblet cells had numerous micro vesicles on the apical surface. The middle stromal layer consisted of well organized collagen fibrils interspersed with stromal cells. A monolayer of flat endothelium-like cells was observed on the inner surface, with a Descemet’s-like basement membrane (Fig. 2B). About 10-15 minicorneas at 8 weeks of differentiation was pooled to isolate total RNA and an RT-PCR analysis revealed the expression of cornea specific markers, PAX6 and $P63$ but not K12. Variant-specific PCR has indicated $P63a$ being the major variant expressed in developing corneas (Fig. 2C).
## Maturation of minicorneas in vitro
Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E) staining of IHC sections of MCs at 6, 8, 10 and 15 weeks of maturation revealed a step-wise process of different tissue layer development and self organization of cells. The transparent MCs grew from $1\ \mathrm{mm}$ to $4~\mathrm{mm}$ in diameter till 10 weeks of differentiation and developed into opaque structures as shown in Fig. 2D, i-iv. The six weeks old transparent MCs under suspension culture (MC-1) consisted of a double layered epithelium and a fluid filled lumen, without any stroma. These structures collapsed immediately after fixation and leakage of internal fluids (Fig. 2D, v). At around 8-10 weeks of differentiation, the MCs got strengthened by sub-epithelial infiltration of spindle-shaped cells (MC-2), resulting in the development of a thick stromal cell layer, which occupied the entire fluid-filled lumen (Fig. 2D, vi). Surprisingly, IHC examination of intact minicorneas developing in situ in adherent cultures revealed complex tissue patterns, with orderly organized layers of different cell types that constitute a normal cornea. The adherent MCs at 10 weeks of differentiation (MC-3) revealed the formation of anterior-segment like structure, consisting of a thin lid-like structure, above a cornea-like tissue (Fig. 2D, vii). At 15 weeks of differentiation, the adherent MCs revealed a mature corneal feature (MC-4), consisting of a well formed corneal and conjunctiva-like surface epithelium, separated by a limbal crypt-like margin zone (Fig. 2D, viii). Pigmented neural crest cells marked the boundary between the clear corneal surface and the surrounding epithelium (Fig. 2D, ix).
## Cornea-specific marker expression patterns in floating corneal organoids
To confirm that the minicornea-like structures are authentic ocular structures and to understand the spatio-temporal distribution of cells within complex tissues, we carried out immunohistochemical examinations on MCs at different stages of development $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{n=8})$ . At 6 weeks, the fragile MC1-1 is made of double-layered epithelium, with fluid filled lumen (Fig. 3A, i). The epithelial cells were $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ PAX6- P63-, suggesting an undifferentiated primitive state (data not shown). Interestingly, a pair of circular niche-like organizer was observed at the connecting base and $\mathrm{Ki}67^{+}$ proliferating cells emerged from there (Fig. 3A, ii). At 8 weeks, the MC1-2 showed significant stromal cell expansion and stratification of surface epithelium. The basal epithelial cells expressed $\mathrm{P}63\mathrm{a}$ and PAX6 (Fig. 3B, i-ii) and the entire stroma was populated by $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ cells (Fig. 3B, iii).  At 10 weeks, the MC-2 developed a thick stratified epithelium, with highly ordered collagen filled stroma. The epithelial cells expressed $\ensuremath{\mathrm{P}}63\ensuremath{\upalpha}$ , PAX6, the cornea-specific cytokeratins, K3/12 and the stromal cells expressed Vimentin (Fig. 3C).
## Characterization of minicorneas developing in situ on adherent EFPs
At 10 weeks, the MC-3 that developed in situ revealed a striking similarity with that of a developing anterior segment, with lid-like structures connected by a periderm-like epithelial lining above the corneal surface, as described elsewhere (Findlater et al., 1993; Huang et al., 2009) (Fig. 4A). The stratified corneal surface epithelium expressed PAX6, $\mathsf{p}63\upalpha$ and low levels of K12; the stromal cells were $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ and the endothelium-like cell layer was $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{CD}200^{+}$ and $\mathrm{GPC4^{+}}$ (Fig. 4B, Fig. S3A). Infiltrating ${\mathrm{\qSMA}}^{+}$ cells were observed within the anterior stroma (Fig. 4B, iv) and the surrounding adnexal cell layers, possibly indicating the development of smooth muscle structures of limbal vasculatures and Schlemn’s canal (Fig. S3B). The stromal, endothelial and lid surface epithelial cells were $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ . But the stratified corneal epithelial cells were VIM- (Fig. 4C, i-ii). Interestingly, a pars plicata-like, ciliary process with pigmented epithelium was observed at the periphery of MC-3, as reported earlier (Kuwahara et al., 2015; Kinoshita et al., 2016). $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ cell clusters flanked the ciliary processes, suggesting the development of trabecular meshwork and choroid-like structures (Fig. 4C, iii-iv).
The 15 weeks old MC-4 was morphologically identical to an adult ocular surface (Fig. 4D), with a distinct limbus-like transition zone separating the $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ , $\mathrm{P}63\mathrm{a}^{+}$ , ${\mathrm{K}}3/12^{+}$ , K10- corneal epithelium on one side and $\mathrm{PAS^{+}}$ and alcian blue+ goblet cell enriched epithelium on the other side (Fig. 4E, Fig. S3C). The cornea-like structure measured about $2\ \mathrm{mm}$ in diameter (approximately 1/6th the size of an adult cornea) and expressed most of the cornea-specific markers observed in adult corneal tissues (Fig. S4). The goblet cells were PAX6-/P63-, which suggested their development from OSE, independent of PAX6 and P63 regulation. Surprisingly, except for a few newly emerging cells, the majority of the goblet cells did not express the adult conjunctival goblet-cell specific mucin, MUC5AC (Fig 4E, vi). Therefore, we further checked for the expression of the other secretory mucin, MUC2. IHC examinations confirmed that the goblet cells were $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{{MUC2^{+}}}}$ (Fig 4E, v). Interspersed between the goblet cells were a few bright $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ and ${\mathrm{K}}19^{+}$ epithelial cells (Fig. 4E, i, Fig. S5B, vii) which suggest the late emergence of conjunctival epithelium and its dependence on PAX6 for development and maturation. Interestingly, a distinct vasculature-like structure with a central lumen and ${\mathrm{\qSMA}}^{+}$ cell lining was observed within the stroma of transition zone. CD34 staining further confirmed the presence of vascular endothelium-like cells on the inner lining of the lumen, thus confirming the initiation of vascular network development along the conjunctival margin (Fig. S5A, vii-viii). Another cluster of spindle shaped $\mathrm{CD}34^{+}$ cells in the peripheral stroma indicated the emergence of mesenchymal cell wave (Fig. S5B, ix).
## A periderm lining for the developing minicorneal structures
Frequent detachment of intact epithelial monolayer on MC surface suggested that they constitute a separate cell layer and possibly of embryonic periderm in origin. Mouse skin periderm cells are
shown to emerge from $\mathrm{P}63+$ surface epithelium during the early stratification events and are P63-, $\mathrm{K}17+$ and ${\mathrm{K}}6+$ cells (Richardson et al., 2014). During development, the periderm layer plays an important role in preventing pathological cell adhesions between the epithelial linings of adjacent organs and ensures normal tissue formation. Our IHC examinations confirmed that the limbal stem cell marker, K15 was exclusively expressed by the basal epithelial cells and K13 marked the surface and supra-basal epithelium as reported earlier (Ramirez-Miranda et al., 2011; Yoshida et al. 2006). Also, the entire epithelium, including the loose surface layers of MC-2 expressed K13 and the periderm markers, K17 and K19 (Fig. 5A). In MC-3, the corneal surface, lid surface and the connecting periderm was lined by ${\mathrm{K}}17^{+}$ and $\mathrm{K}19^{+}$ cells (Fig. 5B). When we examined the adult tissues, we observed that the flat wing cells at the corneal epithelial surface retained K13 and K19 expression, while the entire adult ocular surface epithelium was K17- (Fig. S6).
## Lid and corneal surface epithelial margins in developing organoids
The lid, forniceal and bulbar conjunctiva, limbal and corneal surfaces are lined by a contiguous sheet of epithelium and are distinguished based on minor differences in marker expression and the presence of additional cell types like the conjunctival goblet cells. It is well known that the basal cells of the entire epithelial lining express P63 (Fig. S7, i). However, the eye-specific PAX6 is expressed only by the corneal and conjunctival epithelial cells (Fig. S7, ii). To check if such higher order cellular organization gets established in mature corneal organoids, we examined 15 weeks old minicorneas (MC-5) in long-term suspension cultures. As the organoids matured, the lid structures got expanded simultaneously and occupied the major volume. The basally positioned corneal structure showed a remarkable cellular organization, with surface epithelium and orderly arranged, compact stromal cells resembling that of a mature corneal tissue. Similar to MC1-1, a nichelike organizer formed the origin of ${\mathsf{P}}63^{+}$ cells and they appear to be migrating away from the centre, in an outward spiraling fashion, as a doube-layered epithelium  (Fig. 6, i). The lid epithelium was stratified and the basal cells expressed p63. However, Pax6 expression was limited to the corneal surface epithelium, with very weak or no expression in epithelial cells along the lid margins (Fig. 6, iii). Similarly, K10 expression was restricted to the lid surface epithelium, with negligible expression in the corneal epithelium (Fig. 6, iv). The corneal epithelial basal cells were $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{P}}63^{+}$ , while the mature supra-basal cells were $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ , P63- (Fig. 6, v). Other adnexal structures such as the lentoid bodies derived from OSE could be identified as $\mathrm{PAX6^{high}}$ , αA-Crystallin+, $\mathsf{p}63^{-}$ cell clusters (Fig. 6, ii-iii). Apart from the lens, the surface epithelial cells also expressed αA-Crystallin, as observed in developing mouse eyes (Fig. S7, iii).
## Limbal margin establishment and delayed emergence of conjunctival epithelium
In 15 weeks old adherent MC-4, we observed that ${\mathsf{P}}63^{+}$ basal cells were restricted to the corneal side and $\mathrm{{Muc}}2^{+}$ goblet cells were restricted to the conjunctival side, at the transition zone (Fig. 4E, ii). In agreement with an earlier report (Richardson et al., 2014), upon epithelial stratification, K17 expression got down regulated in the surface periderm cells and the basal epithelial stem cells were ${\mathsf{P}}63^{+}$ and ${\mathrm{K}}17^{+}$ (Fig. 7A, i) and established a clear transition zone resembling that of a limbal margin. The abundance of $\mathrm{Ki}67^{+}$ proliferating cells within the goblet cell enriched epithelium indicates that the progenitor cell proliferation, differentiation and tissue expansion proceeds from the transition zone (Fig. 7A, ii). A dual staining for MUC2 and P63, K3/12 expression further confirmed the presence of a transition zone (Fig 7A, iii-iv). The surface epithelium formed a Collagen IV enriched basement membrane, while the $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ stromal cells laid out a well organized Collagen I enriched extracellular matrix (Fig. 7A, v-vi). When the expression patterns of other epithelial keratins were examined, we observed that ${\mathrm{K}}13^{+}$ , ${\mathrm{K}}15^{+}$ and ${\mathrm{K}}17^{+}$ cells were restricted to the limbal margin. Within the stratified corneal epithelium, K13 marked the surface and suprabasal cells, K15 and K17 marked the basal and supra basal cells (Fig. 7B). However, the entire surface epithelial cells expressed K19, with intense staining in the basal cells, surface periderm-like cells and in few developing conjunctival epithelial cells, which suggested the late emergence of conjunctival epithelium.
## Characterization of cell outgrowths from EFPs
When the cell outgrowths from EFPs were analyzed in adherent 2D cultures, we found that ${\mathrm{SSEA4^{+}}}$ primordial cells tend to organize into ruffled structures resembling the limbal crypts and gave rise to ${\mathsf{P}}63^{+}$ and $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ ocular surface epithelial cells and waves of ${\mathrm{NES}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{SOX10^{+}}$ and week $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ NCCs, (Fig. 7C, i-iii, Video S2, S3). ${\mathrm{NES}}^{+}$ cells are a component of the native limbal niche (Fig. S4, x) and we show that iPSC derived $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ epithelial outgrowths are lined by ${\mathrm{NES}}^{+}$ NCCs that co-expressed PAX6 as reported earlier (Mariappan et al., 2014) (Fig. 7C, iv). Patches of $\mathrm{PAX}6^{\mathrm{low}}$ neural crest-like cells differentiated into corneal endothelium-like phenotype by down regulating PAX6. They appeared as hexagonal shaped, compactly arranged, non-pigmented, flat cells with ${Z}0{-}1^{+}$ tight junctions (Fig. 7D, i-iii). The migratory NCCs were found to be $\mathrm{SOX10^{+}/P63^{-}}$ cells (Fig. 7D, iv).
## Characterization of corneal organoid-derived transplantable cell sheets
Explants cultures of 8-10 weeks old MCs on glass coverslips resulted in a spiraling wave of P63-, ${\mathrm{K}}17^{+}$ ocular surface ectodermal (OSE) cells at the leading edge, followed by compactly arranged monolayer of $\mathrm{P}63^{+/}\mathrm{K}17^{+}$ corneal epithelial (CE) cells (Fig. S8). In an attempt to generate transplantable sheets of corneal epithelium, we established explants cultures on denuded human amniotic membrane (hAM) substrates, using mature cornea-like organoids at 8-10 weeks of maturation. IHC examination of 10 day old cultures confirmed that the uniform sheets of $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ , ${\mathrm{K}}12^{+}$ epithelium generated using corneal organoids were comparable with those generated using adult limbal explants.  The basal epithelial cells were $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{P}}63^{+}$ , ${\mathfrak{p}}7{\bar{5}}^{+}$ and also expressed K17, K19 and Vimentin (Fig. 8).  Interestingly, the resting corneal epithelium was found to be VIM- and K17- (Fig. 4, 6). However, the actively proliferating cells in both the limbal and organoid explants cultures were $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{K}17^{+}$ , suggesting a possible activation of these markers during acute regeneration and wound healing responses.
## Discussion
Autologous iPSC-derived corneal cell source will offer a promising alternative for the treatment of patients with bilateral LSCD. A few earlier reports have shown the possibility of deriving $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ , ${\tt P}63^{+}$ , ${\mathrm{K}}3/12^{+}$ corneal epithelial cells from PSCs in 2D cultures. Here, we report for the first time, an efficient method of generating complex 3D corneal organoids using iPSCs, which resolved the need for complicated cell enrichment procedures involved in establishing limbal cultures.
The adoption of a simple differentiation protocol by the direct shifting of growing cultures to retinal differentiation conditions, in the absence of Noggin has resulted in successful induction of EFP clusters. We also emphasize that it is critical to excise EFPs and initiate suspension cultures at 4 weeks of differentiation, before the commencement of surface ectodermal and neural crest cell migration, to ensure successful induction of corneal organoids in approximately $40\%$ of the EFPs by 6 weeks. We believe that the inhibition of migration of proliferating progenitor cells away from EFPs in suspension culture has enabled the autonomous self assembly of various cell types, resulting in the generation of complex 3D corneal organoids.
The bilayered epithelium of the newly emerged MCs was derived from the primitive periderm like, P63-, PAX6-, $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ OSE cells. The presence of internal fluid appears to help in establishing a circular and convex shape for the developing corneas. The subsequent wave of $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ stromal cells and the deposition of collagen matrix helped to strengthen the outer scaffold of OSE cells. The developing lid-like structure in MC-3 was lined and connected by a continuous periderm like epithelium above the corneal surface. This observation is in agreement with the fact that the developing eye lids fuse and form a continuous covering over the developing cornea. The connecting periderm disintegrates and enables lid separation and eye opening during advanced stages of embryonic development in humans and at post-natal stages in rodents (Findlater et al., 1993; Huang et al., 2009). Unlike the developing skin periderm that is shed after birth (Richardson et al., 2014), the presence of $\mathrm{K}13^{+}$ , $\mathrm{K}17^{+}$ , ${\bf K}19^{+}$ periderm-like surface epithelium in developing MCs and in adult corneas suggests their probable role in normal ocular surface development and in adult tissue homeostasis. We hypothesize that this unique surface lining may help in preventing abnormal cell fusions between the corneal and lid surface epithelium during embryonic eye development and in wound repair processes during adult tissue regeneration.
The observation of ciliary margin zone-like, pigmented and ruffled epithelium, flanked by VIM+   
structures, at the corneal periphery, prompts us to speculate that the CMZ development might precede or coincide with ocular surface periderm formation (around 5-6 weeks). The secretions of the CMZ cells might contribute towards setting the initial corneal shape, which further gets strengthened by the infiltration of $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ neural crest cells. The neural crest cells also contributed to the formation of a monolayer of $\mathrm{VIM^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{CD200^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{GPC4^{+}}$ endotheliumlike cells below the thick stroma, thus resulting in the generation of a complete anterior segment-like structure.
As the minicorneas matured, the lid and the limbal margins got established by the spatiotemporal pattern of expression of P63, PAX6 and keratins. The ${\mathsf{P}}63^{+}$ cells were restricted to the corneal and limbal basal epithelial cells, thus establishing a sharp boundary between the cornea and the future conjunctiva. While the entire surface epithelium expressed PAX6 at low levels, well differentiated central corneal cells and a subset of cells within the conjunctival region were bright $\mathrm{PAX}6^{+}$ , thus confirming its key role in corneal maturation and the emergence of conjunctival epithelium. Majority of the goblet cells on the conjunctival side were $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{{MUC2}^{+}}}$ , P63-, PAX6-, which suggests that the goblet cells emerge from the primitive OSE cells independent of P63 and PAX6 expression. However, the adult conjunctival goblet cells expressed very low levels of MUC2 (McKenzie et al., 2000) and were predominantly MUC5AC+ (Fig. S4A, v). An earlier report has confirmed that the goblet cell development was normal in Muc5ac-/-/Muc5b-/- mice (Marko et al., 2014). Taken together, we believe that MUC2 and MUC5AC are the developing and mature conjunctival goblet cell markers respectively. The presence of niche-like organizing structures consisting of $\mathrm{Ki}67^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{P}}63^{+}$ cells suggests that the tissue growth and expansion proceeds from such transition zones. Further anatomical maturation of corneal tissue was mediated by the infiltration of $\mathrm{CD}34^{+}$ mesenchymal stem cells (Sidney et al., 2014) and other neural crest derived cell types such as the smooth muscle cells, which contributes to the formation of limbal and episcleral vasculatures.
Earlier evidence have confirmed the roles of PAX6 in regulating NCC migration and their differentiation into ocular cell types (Baulmann et al., 2002; Kanakubo et al., 2006) and the involvement of NCC dependant signaling in feedback regulation on PAX6 (Grocott et al., 2011). Our observations indicate that the PAX6low NCCs differentiated into flat, nonpigmented, endothelium-like, hexagonal shaped cells by down regulating PAX6 expression. Explant cultures of 8-10 weeks old MCs on hAM has enabled the generation of transplantable sheets of $\mathrm{Pax}6^{+,}$ ${\mathsf{P}}63^{+}$ , $\mathrm{K}12^{+}$ corneal epithelial sheets, similar to adult limbal tissue derived grafts meant for regenerative applications. We further plan to use these tissue grafts in xenotransplantation studies in rabbit LSCD models, to test their clinical suitability in corneal surface reconstruction procedures.
## Conclusion
In summary, we show for the first time that complex 3D corneal organoids could be generated from iPSCs and the minicorneas undergoes maturation in vitro and recapitulate the normal corneal developmental steps as depicted in Fig. 9. Such minicorneas at 10 weeks of maturation resolves the need for complicated cell enrichment protocols and offers a simpler method of establishing enriched cultures of corneal epithelial cell sheets for basic research needs and for regenerative applications.
